WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.722
Woody: There's no place like home!

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:07.080
[Narrator] This is a dog
in 1995's "Toy Story."

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.500
It was a staggering
achievement at the time,

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:12.300
but the detail in the
fur just isn't there.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.000
Compare that to this cat in "Toy Story 4."

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.330
The difference is clear.

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:17.988
But getting from that dog

00:00:17.988 --> 00:00:18.896
to this cat

00:00:18.896 --> 00:00:21.319
required a lot of innovation in between.

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:30.120
Pixar forever changed animation
in 1995 with "Toy Story,"

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.260
the first full-length
computer-animated movie.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:35.610
With "Toy Story 4," it's proving

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:38.085
that it's far from done changing the game.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:44.640
Between 1995 and 2019,

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.730
Pixar has made 21 feature-length films,

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:50.610
four of those being "Toy Story" movies.

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:53.700
When "Toy Story" was
first released in 1995,

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:56.280
nobody had ever seen
anything like it before.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:57.810
And in order to bring it to life,

00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:00.780
the animators had to do
some unbelievable things.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:02.520
And one of the most important factors

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:06.000
in how Pixar makes its magic
happen involves rendering.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.020
Rendering saves the computer image

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:10.760
to the perfect finished
image or video frame,

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:12.950
with lighting and motion effects.

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:15.390
In order to render "Toy
Story," the animators

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:19.330
had 117 computers running 24 hours a day.

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:22.330
Each individual frame
could take from 45 minutes

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:26.077
to 30 hours to render,
depending on how complex.

00:01:26.077 --> 00:01:27.377
There were a total of

00:01:27.377 --> 00:01:31.209
114,240 frames to render.

00:01:31.209 --> 00:01:34.720
Throughout the movie, there are
over 77 minutes of animation

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:38.540
spread across 1,561 shots.

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:41.600
They had to invent a new
software, called Renderman,

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:43.180
to handle all this footage.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:44.830
Woody: These guys are professionals.

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:45.960
They're the best!

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:48.120
[Narrator] According to
producer Jonas Rivera,

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:50.760
if they had to today, they
could render "Toy Story"

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:53.213
faster than you could
watch the entire movie.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:56.940
However, the complexity of "Toy Story 4"

00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:01.940
means it can take 60 to 160
hours to render one frame.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.150
And there were a lot of limitations.

00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:07.650
For instance, at this time,
Pixar hadn't quite figured out

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:09.890
how to fully animate human characters.

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:12.090
Animating clothes was time-consuming,

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:14.340
so you'll notice a lot of
shots of hands and feet

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:16.470
in the movie from a toy's perspective.

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:18.260
Additionally, they would sometimes choose

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:19.910
not to fully light characters,

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:22.130
so you wouldn't see any missing details.

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:25.830
More on lighting characters
and fully clothing them later.

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:28.440
When "Toy Story 2" came around in 1999,

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:30.840
they'd had some time to
work out some of the kinks,

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:33.320
especially with 1998's "A Bug's Life"

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:34.820
in between the two movies.

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:37.280
In this sequel, you'll be
able to see more visible,

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:39.250
fully formed human characters.

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:41.690
One key thing the animators
were starting to figure out here

00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:44.200
to help them tackle humans: smoothness,

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:46.670
which they got practice
on in "A Bug's Life."

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:49.820
Here, you can see the
improvement in just a few years.

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:52.070
They wouldn't be ready to
have a fully human cast

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:54.520
until 2004's "The Incredibles."

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:57.470
But before mastering humans,
they stepped into fantasy

00:02:57.470 --> 00:02:59.680
with 2001's "Monsters, Inc."

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:02.000
Worker: Ooh, they're so awesome!

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:04.870
[Narrator] In "Monsters, Inc."
they tackled fur head-on.

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:06.070
Fur is hard to animate,

00:03:06.070 --> 00:03:08.700
whether computer-generated or stop-motion.

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:11.300
This is because it involves
animating thousands,

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:15.160
or even millions, of individual
parts of a character's body.

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:17.400
In the VFX world, characters are designed

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:19.650
then rigged by adding points of movement,

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:21.880
which can then be manually manipulated.

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:24.860
While limbs are typically
manual based on the scene,

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:27.060
something like fur needed to be automated,

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:29.090
since it would take a
lot more time to move

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:30.800
each individual strand of hair.

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:33.080
"A Bug's Life," which centers on insects,

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:34.507
didn't have a need for fur.

00:03:34.507 --> 00:03:37.550
And there is a dog in "Toy
Story," but as you'll notice,

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:41.000
it's pretty smooth, as is
the dog in "Toy Story 2."

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:43.720
Sully, who's tall and
covered head to toe in fur,

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:46.100
has over 1 million hairs on his body.

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:47.700
But it's not just how it looks;

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:50.410
the animators had to get
all of those hairs to move.

00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:53.150
To do this, they created a
program called Simulation,

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:54.980
in which certain elements
that are too difficult

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:57.720
to hand-animate are motion-simulated.

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:00.400
See, instead of looking at
Sully's hair as a whole,

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:02.850
they looked at each strand
as a distinct particle.

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:04.730
They had to look at every kind of force

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:06.350
that would act on those particles,

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:09.010
and thus how each one would
move in reaction to them.

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:10.530
So if you want to work at Pixar,

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:12.380
you might need to know physics.

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:15.280
They also found real-world
fur on different animals,

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:17.670
like llamas, for reference.

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:20.350
By doing this, they found
the best way to make hair

00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:23.410
look and move realistically
is to clump it together.

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:26.000
"Monsters, Inc." laid down
the technical foundation,

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:29.230
which allowed Pixar to have
over 250 furry monsters

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:31.610
in the sequel, "Monsters University."

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:33.780
The tools at their
disposal also helped them

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:36.610
create fur on animals seen
in "Ratatouille" and "Up,"

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:39.680
the moss on the submarine from
this scene in "Finding Nemo,"

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:42.598
and the grass on the ground in "Cars."

00:04:42.598 --> 00:04:45.750
Pixar's next movie, 2003's "Finding Nemo,"

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:48.010
also required the
animators to create things

00:04:48.010 --> 00:04:49.970
they'd never put on screen before.

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:52.950
This time, they had to figure
out how to make a movie

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:54.690
set largely underwater.

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:57.140
Once again, science and
real-world references

00:04:57.140 --> 00:04:58.110
would help them out.

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:00.560
Just as they did with hair,
they broke down the water

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:02.370
as much as they could to get it right.

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:05.280
According to director of
photography Danielle Feinberg,

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:07.630
they started with a
real-life underwater clip,

00:05:07.630 --> 00:05:09.330
re-created it in the computer,

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:12.310
and broke it down to find
the most essential elements.

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:15.680
The biggest one? Light, and
how it travels through water.

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:18.420
The light they created
affected both the visibility

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:21.660
and the color of the film's
elaborate underwater world.

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:23.020
And while they need science

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:24.970
to animate certain elements of a movie,

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:27.460
there are times where they
can use artistic license.

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:29.850
For instance, they made
the water in Sydney Harbour

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:32.400
look fairly green to fit
the mood of the scene.

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:34.880
In reality, it would not be that color.

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:36.290
Once the environment was created,

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:37.910
they had to populate the world.

00:05:37.910 --> 00:05:40.780
Perhaps the most challenging
sea creature they had to create

00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:44.820
was Hank the octopus from the
2016 sequel, "Finding Dory."

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:48.210
It was literally impossible
for them to put Hank in "Nemo,"

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:49.360
and you can see why.

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:50.920
Creating just one scene with him

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:52.950
supposedly took about two years.

00:05:52.950 --> 00:05:55.960
Character supervisor
Jeremy Talbot explained

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:57.740
that they had to break down an octopus

00:05:57.740 --> 00:05:59.550
and piece it back together again,

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:02.770
which sounds a lot like how
Pixar mastered fur and water.

00:06:02.770 --> 00:06:05.090
One thing they discovered
was that octopus tentacles

00:06:05.090 --> 00:06:07.590
don't bend but almost unfurl.

00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:09.900
An engineer spent six months just getting

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:11.810
the curve of one of his tentacles right,

00:06:11.810 --> 00:06:14.600
and this was even before
they mastered his camouflage.

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:17.430
And as Pixar got better at
developing the natural world,

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:19.740
it also improved on the man-made world.

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:22.560
By the time 2006's "Cars" came around,

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:25.420
Pixar had about 1,000
times the computing power

00:06:25.420 --> 00:06:26.893
it did on "Toy Story."

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:31.610
"Cars" gave the animators a
chance to hone their skills

00:06:31.610 --> 00:06:33.140
creating metal surfaces.

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:35.340
As they did with the
water in "Finding Nemo,"

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:37.190
they took time to make the light reflect

00:06:37.190 --> 00:06:38.480
off Lightning McQueen.

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:40.800
Those metal surfaces
would then be rusted up

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:43.580
and seen in 2008's
"Wall-E," often considered

00:06:43.580 --> 00:06:46.090
one of Pixar's most
visually stunning works.

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:48.760
Then when "Ratatouille"
rolled around in 2007,

00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:50.180
Pixar combined its ability

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:52.060
to work with fur from "Monsters, Inc."

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:55.070
and with water in "Finding
Nemo" to display wet fur.

00:06:55.070 --> 00:06:57.520
Lighting is one of the
most important factors

00:06:57.520 --> 00:06:59.630
in making CG animation look real.

00:06:59.630 --> 00:07:02.010
It takes a lot of rendering
time to get it right.

00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:04.650
And it's not just one or two
lights we're talking about.

00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:08.850
This one shot alone in
"Ratatouille" contained 230 lights.

00:07:08.850 --> 00:07:10.290
But that's nothing.

00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:12.830
Jump ahead to 2017's "Coco."

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:14.770
When Miguel enters the Land of the Dead,

00:07:14.770 --> 00:07:17.460
he's laying his eyes on
about 8 million lights.

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:20.440
And even with a movie as
visually ambitious as this one,

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:23.690
something as simple as clothes
can be the biggest challenge.

00:07:23.690 --> 00:07:26.100
A lot of the characters
that wore clothes in "Coco"

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:27.570
were actually skeletons.

00:07:27.570 --> 00:07:30.450
The animators found that
while simulating clothing,

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:33.780
the cloth would often get
caught between individual bones,

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:35.700
creating a wedgie of sorts.

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:37.610
For this, it implemented a technique

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:40.060
called continuous collision detection,

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:41.580
which allowed the animators to spot

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:42.770
the clothes getting caught,

00:07:42.770 --> 00:07:45.430
even at moments where it
was difficult to notice.

00:07:45.430 --> 00:07:48.420
A year later, when the long-awaited
"Incredibles 2" came out

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:51.610
they were back to working with
humans: skin, bones, and all.

00:07:51.610 --> 00:07:54.690
There was a 14-year gap between
the two "Incredibles" movies

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:56.680
and the benefits of improved technology

00:07:56.680 --> 00:07:58.610
actually allowed them to make Jack-Jack

00:07:58.610 --> 00:08:01.020
look even cuter than he
did in the first movie.

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:05.380
All of these movies would
eventually lead to "Toy Story 4."

00:08:05.380 --> 00:08:08.390
Twenty-four years after
the original was released,

00:08:08.390 --> 00:08:11.350
it seems like this sequel is
trying to do a lot of things

00:08:11.350 --> 00:08:13.130
the original just couldn't.

00:08:13.130 --> 00:08:15.360
The differences couldn't be more stark.

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:19.000
While "Toy Story" used
a 1.85:1 aspect ratio,

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:22.270
"Toy Story 4" expanded to 2.39:1,

00:08:22.270 --> 00:08:24.537
so what you saw went from this

00:08:24.537 --> 00:08:25.370
to this.

00:08:25.370 --> 00:08:26.700
Now that Pixar had figured out

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:29.540
how to put fur on one character
and then hundreds more,

00:08:29.540 --> 00:08:32.320
it could make the animals
in this movie eye-popping.

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:34.900
Fans pointed out that a
cat in a clip for the movie

00:08:34.900 --> 00:08:36.895
looked 100% real.

00:08:36.895 --> 00:08:38.920
Compare that with Sid's
dog in the original

00:08:38.920 --> 00:08:41.650
if you really want to know
how far Pixar has come.

00:08:41.650 --> 00:08:43.790
Even just compare how
much different it looks

00:08:43.790 --> 00:08:45.130
from the cat in "Coco."

00:08:45.130 --> 00:08:48.220
In the same vein, creating
stuffed animals covered in fur

00:08:48.220 --> 00:08:50.210
was not the challenge it once was,

00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:53.650
and it allowed Pixar to give
Ducky and Bunny starring roles.

00:08:53.650 --> 00:08:56.830
In addition, rain scenes
are not easy to animate.

00:08:56.830 --> 00:08:58.860
Just like with the
water in "Finding Nemo,"

00:08:58.860 --> 00:09:00.800
the animators did a physics-centered,

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:03.070
frame-by-frame analysis of raindrops.

00:09:03.070 --> 00:09:05.070
Luckily, they'd done
enough creating rivers

00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:07.600
in "The Good Dinosaur" that
bringing a torrential downpour

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:09.760
to life was a little less challenging.

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:11.690
Like with Jack-Jack in "Incredibles 2,"

00:09:11.690 --> 00:09:14.020
the animators use sequels as an excuse

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:15.680
to improve upon their classics.

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:17.720
They didn't just give
Bo Peep a new outfit;

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:19.250
they were also able to make her look

00:09:19.250 --> 00:09:21.050
like a much more convincing doll.

00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:23.200
Years of perfecting shiny surfaces

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:26.470
allowed them to really bring
out Bo's porcelain skin.

00:09:26.470 --> 00:09:28.390
To effectively light this material,

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:30.170
the director of photography referenced

00:09:30.170 --> 00:09:32.040
lighting of female characters in movies

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:35.290
made from the 1930s through the 1960s.

00:09:35.290 --> 00:09:37.110
The team also realized they couldn't use

00:09:37.110 --> 00:09:38.410
square lights on Bo.

00:09:38.410 --> 00:09:40.820
Only circular lights without sharp edges

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:42.770
would reflect well on porcelain.

00:09:42.770 --> 00:09:44.990
"Toy Story 4" also gave Pixar a chance

00:09:44.990 --> 00:09:47.930
to do something surprising:
go back to basics.

00:09:47.930 --> 00:09:49.770
Look no further than Forky.

00:09:49.770 --> 00:09:51.400
Forky: I don't belong here!

00:09:51.400 --> 00:09:53.490
[Narrator] There are no tentacles, no fur,

00:09:53.490 --> 00:09:56.440
no shiny surfaces here,
just a plastic fork.

00:09:56.440 --> 00:09:58.210
But he couldn't be more real.

00:09:58.210 --> 00:10:01.260
And Pixar isn't slowing down anytime soon.

00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:04.352
In fact, they're moving
"Onward," literally.

